  <h2>How do I read these with my kid?</h2>
  <p>
    Read the story straight through first, without stopping to explain anything. Then ask
    one open question and let the silence sit. "What do you think Frederick was really
    doing all winter?" works better than "did you learn that thinking is important?" The
    first invites a real answer. The second invites the answer the kid thinks you want.
    For more on how to keep that conversation going past the last page, see{' '}
    <a href="/guides/how-to-introduce-philosophy-to-your-child">
      how to introduce philosophy to your child
    </a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where should I start?</h2>
  <p>
    Ages 3-5: start with <strong>Because of an Acorn</strong> or{' '}
    <strong>The Adventures of the Curious Kid</strong>, both simple enough to hold a
    younger child's attention with a story alone. Ages 5-8: <strong>Zen Shorts</strong>,{' '}
    <strong>Frederick</strong>, and <strong>The Curious Kid Meets Aristotle</strong> all
    land well in this range. Ages 8-10: move to <strong>The Three Questions</strong> and{' '}
    <strong>Big Ideas for Curious Minds</strong>, which ask a little more of the reader.
  </p>

